## Rule 2 — Generate the Keystore Once, Back It Up Twice

Use `keytool` to generate an RSA-2048 (or stronger) keystore valid for at least 25 years. The Play Store requires upload key validity through 2033 minimum.

```bash
# Generate a release keystore — run ONCE per app
keytool -genkeypair \
  -v \
  -keystore example-release.jks \
  -keyalg RSA \
  -keysize 2048 \
  -validity 10000 \
  -alias example-release \
  -storetype PKCS12

# Verify the keystore contents
keytool -list -v -keystore example-release.jks
```

**Backup checklist (do all three):**
- Encrypted password manager entry with keystore password, key alias, key password.
- Encrypted offline backup of the `.jks` file (1Password / Bitwarden vault attachment, or hardware-encrypted USB stored in a safe).
- Use **Play App Signing** in the Play Console so Google holds the production signing key; you only manage the upload key and can rotate it via key reset.

Losing the upload key without Play App Signing enabled means you can never publish updates under the same `applicationId`. Enrol every new app in Play App Signing on first publish.

